    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Foods with a high glycemic index are digested quickly, causing rapid spikes in blood sugar that trigger a sharp release of insulin to manage the glucose surge. Conversely, low-glycemic index foods are broken down more slowly, leading to a gradual rise in blood sugar and a more stable, moderate insulin response. Consequently, high-GI foods can rapidly fluctuate energy levels due to blood sugar crashes, while low-GI foods provide sustained energy and help prevent excessive insulin spikes associated with type 2 diabetes risk.",
